WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.000
In 2015, Earth saw the birth of a new island, the first of its explosive type in 53 years.

00:07.000 --> 00:12.000
The blast was so large that nearby tourists caught the explosion on camera.

00:12.000 --> 00:17.000
Despite raging volcanic activity above and below the Earth's crust, an event like this is pretty rare,

00:17.000 --> 00:21.000
which is why it immediately caught the attention of Dr. Jim Garvin,

00:21.000 --> 00:25.000
chief scientist at NASA's Goddard Space Flight Center and Mars expert.

00:25.000 --> 00:30.000
It could be a pile of basaltic and acidic rocks, that's what you expect in this kind of setting,

00:30.000 --> 00:32.000
but there's more.

00:33.000 --> 00:36.000
What does a Mars expert see in the island that the rest of us don't?

00:41.000 --> 00:44.000
The new island, unofficially known as Hongatonga-Hongahaipei,

00:44.000 --> 00:50.000
is located in the remote southwest Pacific nestled between two other islands in the Kingdom of Tonga.

00:50.000 --> 00:54.000
It's the first island of its kind to erupt and persist in the modern satellite era,

00:54.000 --> 00:58.000
giving scientists an unprecedented view from space of its evolution.

00:59.000 --> 01:02.000
There are other islands being formed, including ones near Japan.

01:02.000 --> 01:04.000
Very nice lava eruptions, classic.

01:04.000 --> 01:07.000
But this one was special because there was this explosive element

01:07.000 --> 01:13.000
that reminded us, at first glance, not exactly of the kind of eruption it surged in.

01:13.000 --> 01:16.000
This is the eruption Jim is talking about.

01:16.000 --> 01:20.000
An island born from a similar explosive eruption in 1963,

01:20.000 --> 01:25.000
and one of only three volcanic islands that have survived in the past 150 years.

01:25.000 --> 01:27.000
Very early in Jim's career,

01:27.000 --> 01:31.000
Cersei was the first newly formed oceanic island he ever studied.

01:34.000 --> 01:37.000
Years later, he went on to become NASA's chief scientist,

01:37.000 --> 01:40.000
pushing the agency's priorities towards Mars exploration

01:40.000 --> 01:44.000
that eventually led to the creation of the Mars Exploration Rovers,

01:44.000 --> 01:48.000
the Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter, and the Mars Science Laboratory.

01:48.000 --> 01:54.000
So why is a scientist clearly fixated on Mars, intrigued by new land on Earth?

01:54.000 --> 01:58.000
The truth is, the two systems are actually cosmically related.

01:58.000 --> 02:02.000
I think these small islands, small volcanic islands, freshly made,

02:02.000 --> 02:08.000
evolving rapidly, are windows into the role of surface waters on Mars

02:08.000 --> 02:11.000
as they have affected small landforms like volcanoes.

02:11.000 --> 02:13.000
And we see fields of them on Mars.

02:13.000 --> 02:15.000
There's a lot to unpack there,

02:15.000 --> 02:18.000
but you can understand the major significance of this on Mars.

02:18.000 --> 02:21.000
You have to understand why it's a big deal on Earth.

02:22.000 --> 02:28.000
It really felt like we were, you know, witnessing something that nobody else had seen.

02:28.000 --> 02:31.000
That's the voice of Dr. Vicky Farini,

02:31.000 --> 02:36.000
one of the first pairs of eyes to see the new island from the deck of her research vessel.

02:37.000 --> 02:42.000
It's this crazy, huge land mass that's sticking about in the water

02:42.000 --> 02:44.000
where we know there wasn't one before.

02:45.000 --> 02:49.000
We watched this island change, and it got more and more exciting.

02:49.000 --> 02:51.000
It didn't wash away.

02:51.000 --> 02:55.000
While there was massive erosion, there was redeposition, protecting the island.

02:55.000 --> 03:01.000
So the initial mass above sea level was eroding very quickly over the first three to six months,

03:01.000 --> 03:05.000
and then it leveled off, so you kind of see a curve, a logarithmic fall off,

03:05.000 --> 03:08.000
and change of that mass above sea level.

03:09.000 --> 03:14.000
Basically, the island dramatically changed shape and size every day for the first few months.

03:14.000 --> 03:17.000
About six months in, it finally stabilized.

03:19.000 --> 03:22.000
Vicky's initial measurements and observations were crucial,

03:22.000 --> 03:27.000
but their research ship couldn't get close to the island without risking a collision.

03:27.000 --> 03:31.000
Two French explorers who were sailing past the island on their worldwide voyage

03:31.000 --> 03:37.000
became NASA's eyes and ears collecting some of the very first images and samples of the interior island.

03:38.000 --> 03:46.000
This is the Earth at its best, because new land, new life, new landscapes, new patterns,

03:46.000 --> 03:48.000
how do they all work together?

03:51.000 --> 03:57.000
The combined observations, satellite images, samples, and three-dimensional topographical maps

03:57.000 --> 04:01.000
led Jim and the team to make some pretty stunning preliminary conclusions.

04:01.000 --> 04:06.000
Scientists think that, in this case, warm seawater interacted with ash after the eruption,

04:06.000 --> 04:10.000
chemically altering the fragile rock into a tougher material.

04:11.000 --> 04:16.000
But studying the life and death of land on Earth also has much broader implications.

04:16.000 --> 04:23.000
This island may give us insights into if and how life formed on Mars in its early history.

04:24.000 --> 04:27.000
Islands like this might have worked on Mars two or three billion years ago,

04:27.000 --> 04:32.000
lakes and small seas, filling depressions, persistent surface waters.

04:32.000 --> 04:40.000
The stuff we really strive to understand because it could have produced the conditions necessary for microbial life or not.

04:41.000 --> 04:46.000
While the verdict is still out on whether or not liquid water on the surface of Mars may have produced life,

04:46.000 --> 04:51.000
scientists are currently running detailed chemical analysis of the island rock samples

04:51.000 --> 04:54.000
that will hopefully provide more answers in the months to come.

04:54.000 --> 04:59.000
Earth is a magical place because really it's our point of departure for everything.

04:59.000 --> 05:06.000
And we've come to realize in the last hundred years or so that it's a far more dynamic world than we ever thought.

05:06.000 --> 05:14.000
Which begs the question, what new secrets will this planet we think we understand so well reveal in the next 100 years?

